WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.702 --> 00:00:07.340
[Michael breathing heavily]

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:09.409
[laughing nervously]

00:00:11.377 --> 00:00:14.313
Everyone is scared
of something.

00:00:14.346 --> 00:00:16.649
But is there some<i> thing</i>

00:00:16.682 --> 00:00:19.752
that everyone is scared of?

00:00:19.785 --> 00:00:24.557
What is the scariest thing
possible?

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.659
♪

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:43.409 line:0%
[Michael]
<i> So what</i> is<i> the scariest thing?</i>

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:47.080 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Is it thunder?
- [thunder crackles]

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:49.082
Shadows?

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.317
Being burned alive?

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:55.822
[laughs nervously]

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:57.824
[laughs evilly]

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:01.761
No. It's also not heights
or needles,

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:03.696
snakes, spiders, sharks--

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:05.765
those things can be scary
to some, sure,

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:07.834
but not to all.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:11.337
So here's what I mean
by "scariest."

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.341
I want a thing--
an object, an action, an idea--

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:20.279 align:start size:88% position:13%
that, at my disposal,
would be guaranteed
to elicit fear and panic

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:23.449
in anyone who might
happen to walk into
this room right now,

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:26.452
regardless of how old they were,
their cultural background,

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:29.856
their abilities,
or even their neurology.

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:33.126
An adventure towards
this ultimate terrible thing

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.663
must necessarily focus
on the womb.

00:01:37.696 --> 00:01:40.133
No, not the kind you came from,

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:44.904
but the womb in which
fear itself gestates: the mind.

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:46.672
-[wind whistling]
-[thunder crashes]

00:01:46.705 --> 00:01:49.175
To find the scariest thing,

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:53.312
we must understand
how fears are born.

00:01:53.345 --> 00:01:55.615
[thunder crashes]

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.783
[projector whirring]

00:01:57.816 --> 00:01:59.385
♪

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.629
Where are we going?

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.797
[laughs evilly]
Don't worry about it.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.566
Only two things matter:

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:17.170
one, I've got plenty of gas,
so what could go wrong?

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:20.806
And two, our destination
is a little bit spooky

00:02:20.839 --> 00:02:22.475
and we're gonna learn.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:24.277
Because in order to truly grasp

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.879
what makes
the one true scariest thing,

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:31.217
well, we need to dive deeper
into how we learn fears.

00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:36.256
[monster growling]

00:02:38.524 --> 00:02:40.393 line:0%
[Michael]
<i> To start out, I commissioned</i>

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.530 line:0%
<i> a fear-conditioning
 demonstration on</i> me. 

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.737
<i> You've probably heard
 that exposure therapy</i>

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.240
<i> can help people
 overcome their fears.</i>

00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:59.712
<i> Well, the same principles
 can be used to create fears.</i>

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:03.583 align:start size:78% position:22%
Hello, Michael?
I'm Dr. Tomislav Zbozinek
from Caltech,

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.685
and today we're gonna do
some fear conditioning.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.253 align:start size:63% position:38%
Do you have
any heart conditions

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:08.888 align:start size:63% position:38%
or any serious major
medical conditions?

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:10.790
- No.
- Okay.

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:12.525 size:78% position:39%
You've done this
to people before.

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:14.460 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- Hundreds of times.
- And they were all okay?

00:03:14.493 --> 00:03:16.462 size:84% position:58% line:0%
They were all okay. Yeah,
everything worked out okay.

00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:19.232 align:start size:88% position:13%
- All right, I'm ready.
- Okay, perfect.

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:21.901
[Michael]<i> The protocol
 for fear conditioning</i>

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:25.504
<i> involves my being electrically
 shocked and startled</i>

00:03:25.537 --> 00:03:27.440
<i> by the sound of a human scream</i>

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:30.276
<i> in connection to visuals
 I see on a screen.</i>

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:31.978
<i> Bioelectric sensors</i>

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:34.914
<i> monitor my body's
 physiological reactions.</i>

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:39.285
<i> For example, my perspiration,
 an indirect measure of fear</i>

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:43.256
<i> that you can't
 consciously control.</i>

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:44.991 align:start position:0%
Is this how you run this
at Caltech?

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:48.427 align:start position:0%
- Yes, this is the exact--
- [laughs] Exactly the same way.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:50.429 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Minus the restraints,
of course.
- Oh, the-- Oh, okay.

00:03:50.462 --> 00:03:53.266 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah. Everything else
is solid fear conditioning--

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:55.401
- As your usual--?
- Yes. Absolutely.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.503 align:start position:0%
All right, I'm game.
Let's help science.

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:01.974
[Michael]
<i> The goal of this procedure</i>

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:04.610
<i> is to condition me
 to be scared of something</i>

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:06.746
<i> I've never feared before--</i>

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:10.349
<i> a mundane, harmless
 geometric shape--</i>

00:04:10.382 --> 00:04:12.952
<i> something normal people
 don't find threatening at all.</i>

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:18.024 align:start size:88% position:13%
- [electricity crackles,
recorded scream]
- Aaaah!

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:26.266 align:start size:88% position:13%
-[electricity crackles]
-[recorded scream]

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:30.536 align:start position:0%
Little bit more scared
of the square right now.

00:04:33.572 --> 00:04:34.874 align:start size:88% position:13%
-[electricity crackles]
-[recorded scream]

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.076
[Michael]
<i> A clear pattern emerges.</i>

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:39.312
<i> My senses are being assaulted</i>

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:42.581
<i> only when the purple square
 is onscreen.</i>

00:04:42.614 --> 00:04:44.417 align:start size:88% position:13%
-[electricity crackles]
-[recorded scream]

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:45.951 size:78% position:39%
[breathing heavily]

00:04:45.984 --> 00:04:48.788
<i> But am I actually
 being conditioned</i>

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:51.057
<i> to</i> fear<i> a purple square?</i>

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:56.028 align:start size:88% position:13%
-[electricity crackles]
-[recorded scream]

00:04:56.061 --> 00:04:57.763 align:start size:59% position:41%
All right, Michael,
you're all done.

00:04:57.796 --> 00:05:00.633 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Hoo!
- How was it?

00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:03.336 align:start position:0%
Well, it felt like
I was one of Pavlov's dogs.

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:05.471 align:start position:0%
I couldn't help
what I was doing,

00:05:05.504 --> 00:05:07.873 align:start position:0%
and I was being trained to do it
in response to something,

00:05:07.906 --> 00:05:09.875 align:start position:0%
and that something was

00:05:09.908 --> 00:05:12.712 align:start position:0%
an otherwise very unassuming
geometric shape.

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:17.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
Exactly. With Pavlov's dogs,
he had a bell and food,
something positive at the end.

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:20.853 size:81% position:59%
But in fear conditioning,
we have something aversive
and negative at the end.

00:05:20.886 --> 00:05:22.421
[Michael]
<i> And it worked.</i>

00:05:22.454 --> 00:05:24.857
<i> Once my brain associated
 the purple square</i>

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:25.858
<i> with being shocked,</i>

00:05:25.891 --> 00:05:28.527 line:0%
<i> my physiological response
 to the square</i>

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.596 line:0%
<i> went up and stayed up.</i>

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:32.865
<i> The mere appearance
 of a simple geometric shape</i>

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:35.534
<i> made me scared enough
 to break a sweat.</i>

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:38.871 align:start position:0%
The results show that you
physiologically really ramped up

00:05:38.904 --> 00:05:40.873 align:start position:0%
to that purple square,
specifically.

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:43.642 align:start position:0%
You quickly learned to be afraid
on a physical level.

00:05:43.675 --> 00:05:44.910 align:start size:50% position:50%
You showed fear.

00:05:44.943 --> 00:05:47.546 align:start position:0%
I came in here today
normal old Michael,

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:49.982 align:start position:0%
but I'm leaving
as a brand-new Michael

00:05:50.015 --> 00:05:52.551 align:start position:0%
who is afraid
of purple squares.

00:05:52.584 --> 00:05:55.788 align:start size:69% position:31%
The human brain
can learn to be afraid
of almost anything.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.959 align:start size:84% position:16%
[Michael]
<i> To better understand
 how this works,</i>

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:03.496
<i> we need to look
 at what's going
 on neurologically.</i>

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:07.933 align:start size:78% position:22%
What happens in the brain
during fear conditioning?

00:06:07.966 --> 00:06:11.170 align:start position:0% line:0%
Well, what we know
is that over evolution,

00:06:11.203 --> 00:06:13.739 align:start position:0% line:0%
over millions of years,

00:06:13.772 --> 00:06:16.108 align:start position:0% line:0%
we've developed these
defensive circuits in our brain.

00:06:16.141 --> 00:06:19.178 align:start position:0% line:0%
And the amygdala sits on
the front of the memory systems

00:06:19.211 --> 00:06:20.746 align:start position:0% line:0%
of the hippocampus.

00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:22.715 align:start position:0% line:0%
And the amygdala seems
to play an important role

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:24.950 align:start position:0% line:0%
in determining
what the danger is

00:06:24.983 --> 00:06:26.685 size:81% position:41% line:0%
of something in the world.

00:06:26.718 --> 00:06:28.421 align:start position:0% line:0%
It tells us what
we should be remembering,

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:32.024 align:start position:0% line:0%
what we should be learning,
that is important to survival.

00:06:32.057 --> 00:06:36.028 align:start size:81% position:19%
[Michael]
<i> Our brain actually
 has two amygdalae--</i>

00:06:36.061 --> 00:06:37.663
<i> one in each hemisphere.</i>

00:06:37.696 --> 00:06:39.131
<i> The function of the amygdalae</i>

00:06:39.164 --> 00:06:41.066
<i> is at the center
 of fear research,</i>

00:06:41.099 --> 00:06:44.603
<i> which covers human behavior
 ranging from the risk averse...</i>

00:06:44.636 --> 00:06:46.639
<i> to high risk-takers</i> 

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:49.175
<i> like free soloist Alex Honnold.</i>

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:53.679
Ancient humans
who avoided danger

00:06:53.712 --> 00:06:56.182
and survived long enough
to reproduce

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:58.484
became our ancestors.

00:06:58.517 --> 00:07:01.086
They populated the world
with creatures like us,

00:07:01.119 --> 00:07:05.157
organisms that instinctively
avoid and are averse to

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.927
potentially dangerous
sensations.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.931
Things like pain and being sick,

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:14.066
suffocation--
the need to breathe.

00:07:14.099 --> 00:07:16.035
We don't think those feel good,

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:18.204
and you don't have to learn
to not like them.

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.673
Even a newborn
is distressed by them,

00:07:20.706 --> 00:07:24.877
which makes them
"innate aversions."

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:28.747
Fear is the anticipation
of these innate aversions.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.884
<i> When the purple square was
 paired with electric shocks,</i>

00:07:31.917 --> 00:07:34.620
<i> my amygdala quickly made
 that association</i>

00:07:34.653 --> 00:07:37.523
<i> and began consciously
 and unconsciously</i>

00:07:37.556 --> 00:07:39.859
<i> arousing fear in me
 whenever I saw it.</i>

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:44.263
For this reason, I want us to
think of sensations as flies

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:47.733
and our amygdala as
a meaty little spider

00:07:47.766 --> 00:07:50.769
spinning a web of fear.

00:07:50.802 --> 00:07:52.238
[thunder crackling]

00:07:56.241 --> 00:08:00.246 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
[Michael]
<i> The web is pre-stocked
 with our innate aversions.</i>

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:04.116 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
<i> Whenever an experience
 is associated with
 an innate aversion,</i>

00:08:04.149 --> 00:08:06.919
<i> it's like a fly
 landing on the web.</i>

00:08:06.952 --> 00:08:09.221 line:0%
<i> This alerts
 the amygdala spider,</i>

00:08:09.254 --> 00:08:11.657 line:0%
<i> which weaves
 a powerful connection</i>

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:13.859 line:0%
<i> between that experience
 and the innate aversion</i>

00:08:13.892 --> 00:08:15.661 line:0%
<i> it's associated with.</i>

00:08:15.694 --> 00:08:18.964
<i> In my case,
 that innate aversion was pain.</i>

00:08:18.997 --> 00:08:21.734
<i> which probably isn't the
 scariest thing for everyone,</i>

00:08:21.767 --> 00:08:24.670
<i> because, well, some people
 have a high pain tolerance.</i>

00:08:24.703 --> 00:08:27.573
<i> Others can learn to suppress</i> 
<i> their fear of pain.</i>

00:08:27.606 --> 00:08:29.575
<i> And, of course,
 some people enjoy pain.</i>

00:08:29.608 --> 00:08:32.578
<i> But the point is,
 now that new experience</i>

00:08:32.611 --> 00:08:34.980
<i> will stay trapped</i> 
<i> in your web of fear,</i>

00:08:35.013 --> 00:08:38.551
<i> a new member of the library
 of things that scare you.</i>

00:08:38.584 --> 00:08:42.054
<i> To find the scariest thing,
 we must wander through</i>

00:08:42.087 --> 00:08:44.256
<i> the darkest recesses
 of the web.</i>

00:08:44.289 --> 00:08:46.759
Everything caught in
your web of fear

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:49.795
is somehow connected
to death.

00:08:49.828 --> 00:08:51.764
Avoiding it--surviving--

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:53.999
is, after all,
what makes fear useful.

00:08:54.032 --> 00:08:57.002
It's why we are still here
today as a species.

00:08:57.035 --> 00:09:02.274 align:start size:88% position:13%
So if death is at
the very center of
all of our webs of fear,

00:09:02.307 --> 00:09:06.779
does that make death
the scariest thing?

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:11.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
[Michael]
<i> Well, although many people
 say death is their #1 fear,</i>

00:09:11.683 --> 00:09:13.786
<i> not everyone is afraid of it.</i>

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:16.589
<i> Many acts of courage require
 caring more about others</i>

00:09:16.622 --> 00:09:18.257
<i> than your own life.</i>

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.193
<i> Some older people say
 they're ready to die.</i>

00:09:21.226 --> 00:09:23.929
<i> And what about those
 who commit suicide?</i>

00:09:23.962 --> 00:09:27.066
<i> Sadly, for them,
 something else was scarier</i>

00:09:27.099 --> 00:09:29.768
<i> than ending their own life.</i>

00:09:29.801 --> 00:09:33.172
So death isn't exactly
the answer we're looking for.

00:09:33.205 --> 00:09:37.977 align:start size:88% position:13%
The scariest thing will
be something else on all
of our webs

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:43.182
that is panic-inducing
even to those who want to die.

00:09:46.818 --> 00:09:48.220
[projector whirring]

00:09:49.855 --> 00:09:52.891
[car rattling]

00:09:52.924 --> 00:09:56.362 align:start size:81% position:19%
Dangnabbit!
Wouldn't ya know it?
I've run out of gas.

00:09:56.395 --> 00:09:59.898 align:start size:91% position:9%
Guess I'm just gonna
have to walk down this
desolate, foreboding road

00:09:59.931 --> 00:10:01.033
in search of a gas station.

00:10:03.068 --> 00:10:05.838
Luckily, this is just a movie.

00:10:05.871 --> 00:10:08.674
My rational mind
knows that I'm safe,

00:10:08.707 --> 00:10:11.811
and it will use that to inhibit
my amygdala's fear response.

00:10:13.111 --> 00:10:15.180 line:0%
[laughs]
What a great way

00:10:15.213 --> 00:10:17.416 line:0%
to make a horror film scarier,
right?

00:10:17.449 --> 00:10:20.052 line:0%
Take away one of
the rational mind's shields,

00:10:20.085 --> 00:10:23.088
and the amygdala's fear response
won't be as inhibited.

00:10:23.121 --> 00:10:25.724
What we're about to see
really could happen,

00:10:25.757 --> 00:10:27.893
and perhaps...will.

00:10:30.062 --> 00:10:33.065 align:start size:91% position:9%
[Michael]
<i> Of course,
 we don't just learn fears</i>

00:10:33.098 --> 00:10:36.168
<i> from firsthand experiences.</i>

00:10:36.201 --> 00:10:40.239
<i> Throughout our history,
 we have used images and words</i>

00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:42.941
<i> to teach fears to one another,</i>

00:10:42.974 --> 00:10:46.979
<i> to prepare the next generation
 for various dangers.</i>

00:10:47.012 --> 00:10:48.781 line:0%
[narrator]
<i> These high school boy and girls</i>

00:10:48.814 --> 00:10:51.383 line:0%
<i> are having a hop
 at the local soda fountain,</i>

00:10:51.416 --> 00:10:53.886 line:0%
<i> innocent of a new
 and deadly menace</i>

00:10:53.919 --> 00:10:55.888 line:0%
<i> lurking behind closed doors.</i>

00:10:55.921 --> 00:10:59.024
<i> Marijuana! The burning weed</i>

00:10:59.057 --> 00:11:02.161
<i> with its roots in hell.</i>

00:11:02.194 --> 00:11:06.398 align:start size:63% position:38%
Why do humans
have to tell stories
to share fears

00:11:06.431 --> 00:11:09.268 align:start size:91% position:9%
from generation to generation
and person to person?

00:11:09.301 --> 00:11:11.437 size:88% position:44% line:0%
Language is virtual reality.

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:14.273 align:start position:0% line:0%
So when you
tell someone a story,

00:11:14.306 --> 00:11:17.376 size:81% position:41%
you're not just describing
a crocodile, for example,

00:11:17.409 --> 00:11:21.447 align:start position:0%
the language in the story
has a kind of emotional impact
on the listener.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:24.750 align:start position:0%
So when I tell you some
scary story about crocodiles,

00:11:24.783 --> 00:11:27.886
the amygdala is being activated
by the words and the scenarios,

00:11:27.919 --> 00:11:30.322 align:start position:0%
allowing the imagination
to sort of play

00:11:30.355 --> 00:11:33.792
with these ideas and scenarios,
so you're concocting imagery.

00:11:33.825 --> 00:11:35.294 size:81% position:41%
So it has a double whammy.

00:11:35.327 --> 00:11:37.162 align:start position:0%
And you're getting
a very good sense

00:11:37.195 --> 00:11:40.099 align:start position:0%
that you should stay away
from this kind of thing
in the future.

00:11:40.132 --> 00:11:44.369 size:81% position:41%
As a species, we're very
dependent and vulnerable
compared to other animals.

00:11:44.402 --> 00:11:48.273 align:start position:0%
We don't have
sharp teeth and big claws,
and we're not really fast--

00:11:48.306 --> 00:11:51.844 align:start position:0% line:0%
it takes us years and years
to be self-sufficient.

00:11:51.877 --> 00:11:54.179 size:81% position:41% line:0%
So what's happening during
those years of development

00:11:54.212 --> 00:11:58.183 align:start position:0% line:0%
is that we're getting
all this information about
the environment through stories.

00:11:58.216 --> 00:12:02.521 align:start position:0%
And part of that
is to know what to be afraid of
and what to not be afraid of.

00:12:02.554 --> 00:12:05.124
[projector whirring]

00:12:05.157 --> 00:12:07.025
[echoing]
Hello!

00:12:07.058 --> 00:12:09.795
-Hey, hello!
-[bird cawing]

00:12:09.828 --> 00:12:12.898
Whoo. Okay, being alone
is kind of scary, right?

00:12:12.931 --> 00:12:17.002 align:start size:88% position:13%
But you know what?
I'm actually not alone.
I am always connected.

00:12:17.035 --> 00:12:19.304
-<i> [ominous motif plays]</i> 
- No reception. Of course.

00:12:19.337 --> 00:12:22.307
I mean, what is this,
a horror movie? Yes, it is.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:24.977 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you know what,
I'm actually getting
a little bit scared right now.

00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:28.814
A new fear, by the way,
has developed in just
the last few decades.

00:12:28.847 --> 00:12:31.049
You see, a while ago,
no one had a cell phone.

00:12:31.082 --> 00:12:33.285
No one was ever connected
through the internet constantly.

00:12:33.318 --> 00:12:35.988
But now many of us,
most of us, are.

00:12:36.021 --> 00:12:37.923
And so that's the new normal,

00:12:37.956 --> 00:12:40.192
and what's abnormal now
is<i> not</i> being connected,

00:12:40.225 --> 00:12:41.994
not having your phone.

00:12:42.027 --> 00:12:43.862
Psychologists
give that fear a name,

00:12:43.895 --> 00:12:45.464
it's a real thing
that's being studied,

00:12:45.497 --> 00:12:48.400 line:0%
and it's called nomophobia.

00:12:48.433 --> 00:12:50.135 line:0%
Anyway, the point is,

00:12:50.168 --> 00:12:53.138 line:0%
the scariest thing possible--
hmm, what could it be?

00:12:53.171 --> 00:12:56.441
Well, an important ingredient
might be...

00:12:56.474 --> 00:12:58.477
[fly buzzing]

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:02.981
<i> ...our innate aversion
 to isolation.</i>

00:13:03.014 --> 00:13:05.317 align:start size:53% position:47%
Why are we afraid
of being alone?

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:07.419 line:0%
Human beings are social animals.

00:13:07.452 --> 00:13:09.321 line:0%
We need each other to survive.

00:13:09.354 --> 00:13:12.324 align:start position:0% line:0%
So if we're alone, it lessens
our chances of survival.

00:13:12.357 --> 00:13:14.393 align:start position:0%
And there's been
a lot of research now

00:13:14.426 --> 00:13:17.329 align:start position:0%
that shows that when people
isolate socially,

00:13:17.362 --> 00:13:20.098 align:start position:0%
it actually leads to things
like increased heart problems,

00:13:20.131 --> 00:13:23.836 align:start position:0%
increased cancer risk,
physical issues, mortality.

00:13:23.869 --> 00:13:28.841 align:start position:0%
It is very much directly tied
to the sense of survival
and avoidance of death.

00:13:28.874 --> 00:13:31.176 align:start size:72% position:28%
What I find interesting
about isolation is,

00:13:31.209 --> 00:13:32.945 size:84% position:58%
I think it helps us capture

00:13:32.978 --> 00:13:36.081 align:start size:88% position:13%
a lot of tertiary-and-beyond
fears

00:13:36.114 --> 00:13:39.351 align:start size:88% position:13%
that don't seem to be
directly connected to death,

00:13:39.384 --> 00:13:42.221 align:start size:63% position:38%
but<i> are,</i> in a way,
through isolation.

00:13:42.254 --> 00:13:45.324 align:start size:88% position:13%
For instance,
the fear of public speaking.

00:13:45.357 --> 00:13:48.327 align:start position:0%
Yes. Well, that taps
into your fear of rejection,

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:51.964 align:start position:0%
and when you feel like
people don't like you,
you feel alone!

00:13:51.997 --> 00:13:54.066 align:start position:0%
And so putting yourself
out there,

00:13:54.099 --> 00:13:57.102 align:start position:0%
having that possible fear
of rejection

00:13:57.135 --> 00:13:59.271
when you're speaking in public

00:13:59.304 --> 00:14:00.906 align:start position:0%
and not knowing how people
are gonna perceive you,

00:14:00.939 --> 00:14:02.241 align:start position:0%
that's very scary
for a lot of people.

00:14:02.274 --> 00:14:04.243 align:start size:56% position:44%
Which doesn't
necessarily mean

00:14:04.276 --> 00:14:06.478 size:84% position:58%
that you will directly die.

00:14:06.511 --> 00:14:11.016 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
However, it connects
to our innate aversion
to isolation

00:14:11.049 --> 00:14:14.953 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
which, through evolution,
we have learned is a bad thing
that can lead to death.

00:14:14.986 --> 00:14:18.023 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- And so we're afraid of it,
avoid it.
- Absolutely.

00:14:18.056 --> 00:14:20.592
In the very first episode
of<i> Mind Field,</i>

00:14:20.625 --> 00:14:24.263
I spent 72 hours
in an isolation chamber.

00:14:24.296 --> 00:14:28.267 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> For three days
 I had no contact
 with the outside world.</i>

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:32.437
<i> I had no clock or window,
 and the lights never went out.</i>

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:35.073
The scariest part, though,
wasn't being alone.

00:14:35.106 --> 00:14:38.210
<i> I could handle that.
 The worst part</i>

00:14:38.243 --> 00:14:41.046 align:start size:88% position:13%
was being separated
from the natural cycles
of the Earth

00:14:41.079 --> 00:14:42.381
I was so accustomed to.

00:14:42.414 --> 00:14:45.584
<i> Night and day. Time.</i>

00:14:45.617 --> 00:14:49.354
<i> The disorientation
 and helplessness caused by that</i>

00:14:49.387 --> 00:14:51.123
<i> made me distressed,</i>

00:14:51.156 --> 00:14:52.391
and may have been a reason why,

00:14:52.424 --> 00:14:55.427
while in the room,
all of my dreams

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:57.262
also took place
in the same room.

00:14:57.295 --> 00:14:59.331
<i> I soon became unable
 to tell the difference</i>

00:14:59.364 --> 00:15:01.500
<i> between when I was dreaming
 and when I was awake.</i>

00:15:01.533 --> 00:15:03.969
<i> I was terrified.</i>

00:15:04.002 --> 00:15:07.472
Extreme isolation like that
is not normal or healthy,

00:15:07.505 --> 00:15:09.508
but we all respond differently.

00:15:09.541 --> 00:15:12.110
There are people who love
living by themselves

00:15:12.143 --> 00:15:13.445
in the middle of nowhere.

00:15:13.478 --> 00:15:16.048
And with sufficient forewarning
of the effects,

00:15:16.081 --> 00:15:18.016
I think I could have
handled it better.

00:15:18.049 --> 00:15:19.484
So just being all alone

00:15:19.517 --> 00:15:22.454
probably isn't
the scariest thing.

00:15:22.487 --> 00:15:25.190
So then what is?

00:15:25.223 --> 00:15:28.493
[projector whirring]

00:15:28.526 --> 00:15:31.497
Ah. You know what
would make this scarier?

00:15:33.598 --> 00:15:35.734
Perfect.

00:15:35.767 --> 00:15:38.670
[wolf howling]

00:15:38.703 --> 00:15:40.172
[fly buzzing]

00:15:41.606 --> 00:15:44.076 align:start size:53% position:47%
Let's talk about
fear of the dark.

00:15:44.109 --> 00:15:48.146 align:start size:63% position:38%
Children and adults,
and myself, often,
don't like the dark.

00:15:48.179 --> 00:15:49.548
- No.
- Why?

00:15:49.581 --> 00:15:51.249 size:75% position:38%
The sensory deprivation.

00:15:51.282 --> 00:15:54.319 size:78% position:39%
It leads you to feel like
you're out of control,

00:15:54.352 --> 00:15:57.189 align:start position:0% line:0%
like you don't know
what's gonna be happening.

00:15:57.222 --> 00:15:58.490 align:start position:0% line:0%
And, at the same time,

00:15:58.523 --> 00:16:00.692 align:start position:0%
predators tend to
come out at night,

00:16:00.725 --> 00:16:03.228 align:start position:0%
so when we think back
to caveman days,

00:16:03.261 --> 00:16:06.331 align:start position:0%
they tend to attack
later on in the day
when you can't see them.

00:16:06.364 --> 00:16:10.402 align:start position:0%
And so there's a natural
inclination for us
to fear the dark,

00:16:10.435 --> 00:16:12.537 align:start position:0%
because we don't know
what's lurking out there

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.173 size:84% position:42%
and there's a lot of things
that can come and hurt us.

00:16:15.206 --> 00:16:16.708 align:start size:59% position:41%
My wife is pregnant
right now.

00:16:16.741 --> 00:16:19.444 align:start size:75% position:25%
I mean, it's pretty dark
inside my wife.

00:16:19.477 --> 00:16:21.313 align:start size:88% position:13%
Is my kid scared of the dark
in there?

00:16:21.346 --> 00:16:22.781 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Nope. Nope.
- Why?

00:16:22.814 --> 00:16:24.750 align:start position:0%
It's comforting for them.
And in fact,

00:16:24.783 --> 00:16:27.452 align:start position:0%
if a baby is colicky,
they can't be soothed,

00:16:27.485 --> 00:16:30.722 align:start position:0%
if you put them in a dark room,
they calm down.

00:16:30.755 --> 00:16:33.058 align:start position:0%
In fact,
this fear of darkness

00:16:33.091 --> 00:16:35.761 align:start position:0%
doesn't really come out
for most children

00:16:35.794 --> 00:16:38.063 size:84% position:42%
until about the age of two.

00:16:38.096 --> 00:16:41.233 align:start position:0%
That's when, I think,
they start to develop
certain cognitive

00:16:41.266 --> 00:16:43.435 size:88% position:44%
and neural memories and maps

00:16:43.468 --> 00:16:45.737 align:start position:0%
about things that might
be scary about the dark.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:49.041 align:start position:0%
For example, your parents
tend not to be in bed with you
necessarily.

00:16:49.074 --> 00:16:51.343 align:start position:0%
They're gone.
So these are the people
who were protecting you.

00:16:51.376 --> 00:16:53.178 align:start position:0%
Well, it's dark,
you're going to bed

00:16:53.211 --> 00:16:55.313 size:78% position:39%
and they go to their room
and you're by yourself.

00:16:55.346 --> 00:16:59.284 align:start size:81% position:19%
So to answer the question,
"What's the scariest thing
possible,"

00:16:59.317 --> 00:17:02.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
the darkness isn't necessarily
the best one, 'cause
if a newborn walked in--

00:17:02.454 --> 00:17:04.589 size:78% position:61%
well, I don't know
how they're walking,
but just go with me--

00:17:04.622 --> 00:17:08.260 align:start size:72% position:28%
a newborn walks in,
it's not gonna be like,
"Aah! It's dark!"

00:17:08.293 --> 00:17:10.062 align:start size:66% position:34%
They'll just be like,
"Hmm."

00:17:10.095 --> 00:17:11.797
Right. "Hmm, it's kinda nice."

00:17:11.830 --> 00:17:14.733 align:start position:0%
And you know who else
isn't scared of the dark?
A blind person.

00:17:14.766 --> 00:17:18.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
Exactly. So darkness,
I'm crossing that off my list.

00:17:18.103 --> 00:17:19.438 align:start size:88% position:13%
Not universal.

00:17:19.471 --> 00:17:22.107 align:start size:84% position:16%
- [projector whirring]
- [wolf howling]

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:25.444 align:start size:97% position:3%
[Michael]
<i> So if messing with our sense
 of sight isn't scary enough,</i>

00:17:25.477 --> 00:17:28.080
<i> what about
 our sense of hearing?</i>

00:17:28.113 --> 00:17:30.082
[woman screaming]

00:17:37.655 --> 00:17:40.692
Okay,
I'm pretty scared right now.

00:17:40.725 --> 00:17:42.694
But I could be more scared.

00:17:42.727 --> 00:17:47.332
[man screaming manically]

00:17:47.365 --> 00:17:49.835
[evil laughter]

00:17:49.868 --> 00:17:54.372
[chorus of screaming, cackling]

00:17:54.405 --> 00:17:59.244 align:start size:59% position:41%
Why is it so easy
to condition a fear
using sound?

00:17:59.277 --> 00:18:02.414 align:start position:0% line:0%
Sound has a specific
neural pathway in the brain

00:18:02.447 --> 00:18:04.716 align:start position:0%
which goes from
the auditory cortex,

00:18:04.749 --> 00:18:07.752 align:start position:0%
thalamus, straight
into the amygdala.

00:18:07.785 --> 00:18:09.855 align:start position:0%
Now, the funny thing
about sound is,

00:18:09.888 --> 00:18:12.491 align:start position:0%
our visual system
allows us to see a threat

00:18:12.524 --> 00:18:15.760 align:start position:0%
and prepare for it,
but sounds don't.

00:18:15.793 --> 00:18:18.563 align:start position:0%
The rustle of some leaves, 
the footsteps in the distance,

00:18:18.596 --> 00:18:20.732 align:start position:0%
evokes anxiety,

00:18:20.765 --> 00:18:23.869
it evokes a situation which
we don't know how to respond,

00:18:23.902 --> 00:18:26.738 align:start position:0%
because we've not seen
a threat yet, we don't know
what it is.

00:18:26.771 --> 00:18:28.306 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Much less information.
- Yes.

00:18:28.339 --> 00:18:30.242
[thunder crashing]

00:18:30.275 --> 00:18:32.277 align:start size:78% position:22%
- [animal growls]
- [owl hoots]

00:18:32.310 --> 00:18:36.414 align:start position:0% line:0%
Sound is a very immediate
processing within the brain.

00:18:36.447 --> 00:18:40.285 align:start position:0%
So a loud sound
creates a startle
for anybody, immediately.

00:18:40.318 --> 00:18:42.821 align:start size:88% position:13%
- staccato chord plays]
- But also some of these
eerie sounds

00:18:42.854 --> 00:18:46.324 align:start position:0%
that you'll hear, they make
the hair on the back
of your neck stand up,

00:18:46.357 --> 00:18:50.862 align:start position:0%
it's basically hijacking
your fear system very directly.

00:18:50.895 --> 00:18:53.565 line:0%
[ethereal noise echoing]
<i> Ch-ch-ch, ah-ah-ah...</i>

00:18:53.598 --> 00:18:56.334 line:0%
<i> ch-ch-ch, ah-ah-ah...</i>

00:18:56.367 --> 00:18:58.904 align:start position:0% line:0%
[Patrick Brice]
<i> The first time I heard
 the "ch-ch-ch...</i>

00:18:58.937 --> 00:19:03.208 size:84% position:42% line:0%
ah-ah-ah, ch-ch-ch," from
the<i> Friday the 13th</i> movies,

00:19:03.241 --> 00:19:04.843 size:88% position:44%
<i> that stuck with me forever.</i>

00:19:06.578 --> 00:19:08.647 align:start size:72% position:28%
- [gasping]
- [man groans]

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.515
<i> [thunder crashing]</i>

00:19:10.548 --> 00:19:13.285 line:0%
<i> [bucolic music playing]</i>

00:19:13.318 --> 00:19:17.956 align:start position:0% line:0%
[Sean Cunningham] 
<i> Music tells you how
 you're supposed to feel.</i>

00:19:17.989 --> 00:19:20.425
<i> [slashing violins playing]</i>

00:19:20.458 --> 00:19:22.394
[screaming]

00:19:22.427 --> 00:19:25.697 align:start position:0% line:0%
And I believe
that we are hardwired
to respond to music.

00:19:28.333 --> 00:19:30.402
<i> In dramatic musical writing,</i>

00:19:30.435 --> 00:19:32.938 align:start position:0%
<i> you can use all kinds
 of different devices...</i>

00:19:32.971 --> 00:19:34.539 align:start size:91% position:9%
-<i> [ethereal sounds]</i> 
- Must be my imagination.

00:19:34.572 --> 00:19:36.708 align:start position:0%
<i> ...to create fear.</i>

00:19:38.409 --> 00:19:41.613 align:start size:91% position:9%
-<i> [slashing violins play]</i> 
- [screams]

00:19:41.646 --> 00:19:45.217
Here I am
walking through the woods.

00:19:47.252 --> 00:19:50.288
And here I am
walking through the woods...

00:19:50.321 --> 00:19:52.457
with scary music.

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:56.261
<i> [violin playing
 suspenseful motif]</i>

00:19:56.294 --> 00:19:58.597
[Michael]
<i> But if one note is scary...</i>

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:00.298
<i> [introduces
 dissonant harmonies]</i>

00:20:00.331 --> 00:20:02.734
<i> ...two notes
 can be even scarier,</i>

00:20:02.767 --> 00:20:05.937
<i> especially
 if they're dissonant.</i>

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:08.340
[Dr. Asma]
<i> There are certain intervals,</i>

00:20:08.373 --> 00:20:10.475 align:start position:0%
<i> two notes that are clashing
 with each other,</i>

00:20:10.508 --> 00:20:12.410
<i> that universally bother people.</i>

00:20:12.443 --> 00:20:15.880 align:start position:0%
They put you in a kind of almost
fight-or-flight situation.

00:20:15.913 --> 00:20:17.749
[Michael]
<i> Musical intervals that fall</i>

00:20:17.782 --> 00:20:19.784
<i> outside of conventional
 harmonies</i>

00:20:19.817 --> 00:20:22.821
<i> may trigger our innate aversion
 to things that are different</i>

00:20:22.854 --> 00:20:24.656
<i> or abnormal.</i>

00:20:24.689 --> 00:20:27.025
[dissonance continues]

00:20:27.058 --> 00:20:30.662
<i> This interval,
 known as the "Devil's Tritone,"</i>

00:20:30.695 --> 00:20:33.498
<i> is so dissonant that composers
 purposefully use it</i>

00:20:33.531 --> 00:20:35.267
<i> to make listeners
 uncomfortable,</i>

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:37.736
<i> from TV themes
 like "The Twilight Zone..."</i>

00:20:37.769 --> 00:20:39.504
<i> [guitars play
 dissonant harmony]</i>

00:20:39.537 --> 00:20:40.872
<i> ...to hard rock like the start</i>

00:20:40.905 --> 00:20:43.308
<i> of Jimi Hendrix's
 "Purple Haze."</i>

00:20:43.341 --> 00:20:45.310
<i> [guitar playing tritone motif]</i>

00:20:45.343 --> 00:20:47.646
[raven cawing]

00:20:47.679 --> 00:20:51.383
<i> But music doesn't always
 have to start out scary.</i>

00:20:51.416 --> 00:20:55.587
The right context
can make even the happiest song

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:58.490
absolutely terrifying.

00:20:58.523 --> 00:21:02.294
[dissonant version of
"Pop Goes the Weasel" playing]

00:21:06.631 --> 00:21:09.501
[music continues,
girl humming along]

00:21:15.873 --> 00:21:18.810
[evil laughter]

00:21:18.843 --> 00:21:21.980 align:start position:0%
Once a song
has been conditioned
to elicit fear,

00:21:22.013 --> 00:21:24.949 align:start position:0%
the effect can be
extremely strong
and long-lasting.

00:21:24.982 --> 00:21:29.020 align:start position:0%
For example, the theme song
from "The Exorcist,"
"Tubular Bells."

00:21:29.053 --> 00:21:31.589 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
<i> Terrifying, right?
 Go listen to it,
 it's scary.</i>

00:21:31.622 --> 00:21:34.092 line:0%
<i> But just a few months
 before the movie came out,</i>

00:21:34.125 --> 00:21:37.495 line:0%
<i> it was a chart-topping
 pop hit that audiences loved</i>

00:21:37.528 --> 00:21:39.698 line:0%
<i> because it was lighthearted
 and fun.</i>

00:21:40.765 --> 00:21:43.568
Oh, strawberry. Very good.

00:21:43.601 --> 00:21:45.837
But here's the problem
with sound.

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:50.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
Not everyone has been
conditioned to be afraid
of the same sounds or music,

00:21:50.041 --> 00:21:53.411
and not everyone can hear--
what about the deaf?

00:21:53.444 --> 00:21:56.848
Clearly, the scariest thing
possible to everyone

00:21:56.881 --> 00:22:00.418
- will not be a sound. 
<i> - [girl laughs evilly]</i>

00:22:00.451 --> 00:22:02.787
Take a look at these paintings.

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:05.457
We have a flirty little clown,

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:08.393
an abstract piece,
and a landscape.

00:22:08.426 --> 00:22:10.762
Now, these paintings
may seem a little bit strange,

00:22:10.795 --> 00:22:12.864
but I wouldn't say
I'm freaked out by them,

00:22:12.897 --> 00:22:14.933
and I doubt you would either.

00:22:14.966 --> 00:22:17.669
But now let me give you
a bit more story--

00:22:17.702 --> 00:22:21.039
the context behind these items.

00:22:21.072 --> 00:22:24.075
This picture was painted
by John Wayne Gacy,

00:22:24.108 --> 00:22:26.845
a serial killer
who tortured, raped,

00:22:26.878 --> 00:22:30.648
and killed 33 teenager boys
and young men.

00:22:30.681 --> 00:22:33.918
This was painted
by Chares Manson,

00:22:33.951 --> 00:22:36.855
the infamous cult leader
who convinced his followers

00:22:36.888 --> 00:22:40.892 align:start position:0%
to commit a series of nine
horrific murders in Los Angeles.

00:22:40.925 --> 00:22:43.461
And this landscape
was painted by...

00:22:43.494 --> 00:22:45.163
Adolf Hitler.

00:22:45.196 --> 00:22:48.133
Your thoughts about these items
have probably changed a bit.

00:22:48.166 --> 00:22:50.502 line:0%
Psychologists call
this phenomenon

00:22:50.535 --> 00:22:52.837 line:0%
the "Law of Contagion."

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:55.140
It's our tendency
to imbue objects

00:22:55.173 --> 00:22:57.909
with the qualities of the people
they're associated with.

00:22:57.942 --> 00:23:00.445
Now, before and after
you heard the story,

00:23:00.478 --> 00:23:03.114
the items were still made
of the same molecules and atoms,

00:23:03.147 --> 00:23:05.583
but our minds use context

00:23:05.616 --> 00:23:07.986
to quickly endow them
with new powers.

00:23:08.019 --> 00:23:10.855
Now, these paintings
cannot hurt you,

00:23:10.888 --> 00:23:14.692 line:0%
but the fact that the context
allows your amygdala spider

00:23:14.725 --> 00:23:16.895 line:0%
to connect them
to other fears and,

00:23:16.928 --> 00:23:20.165 line:0%
ultimately, through
your innate aversions, to death,

00:23:20.198 --> 00:23:23.701 line:0%
means they take on
a whole new meaning.

00:23:23.734 --> 00:23:26.604
<i> The right story and context</i>

00:23:26.637 --> 00:23:30.475
<i> can make almost any object,
 person, or place scary.</i>

00:23:30.508 --> 00:23:32.977
In fact, let me
ask you a question.

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:35.680
What is this, behind me?

00:23:38.783 --> 00:23:40.485
Ah, you know what?

00:23:40.518 --> 00:23:43.855
This old camper
isn't actually that scary.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:47.959
Ahh, nuts.

00:23:47.992 --> 00:23:50.628
All right, things
are different now,

00:23:50.661 --> 00:23:52.497
and I'm starting to feel, yep,

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:55.099
an automatic process
is already happening to my body.

00:23:55.132 --> 00:24:00.171 align:start size:88% position:13%
My amygdala now
associates this trailer
with death and danger.

00:24:00.204 --> 00:24:02.874
What I'm feeling is actually
the same thing that happens

00:24:02.907 --> 00:24:04.776 line:0%
when you feel frisson.

00:24:04.809 --> 00:24:08.179 line:0%
That's when you're greatly moved
by something profound.

00:24:08.212 --> 00:24:11.049
<i> What's happening
 is that my amygdala</i>

00:24:11.082 --> 00:24:14.118
<i> is telling my hypothalamus</i> 
<i> to tell my adrenal glands</i>

00:24:14.151 --> 00:24:17.088
<i> to start pumping adrenaline
 into my bloodstream.</i>

00:24:17.121 --> 00:24:20.658
<i> This makes my arrector pili
 muscles contract,</i>

00:24:20.691 --> 00:24:22.894
<i> which makes my hairs stand up.</i>

00:24:22.927 --> 00:24:26.164
<i> Now, if my body were covered
 in thick fur like, say a cat,</i>

00:24:26.197 --> 00:24:29.067
<i> this would make me look bigger
 and more threatening.</i>

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.169
<i> But of course,
 a long, long time ago,</i>

00:24:31.202 --> 00:24:33.738
<i> we humans lost
 a lot of our fur.</i>

00:24:33.771 --> 00:24:35.106
<i> But we didn't lose this reflex,</i>

00:24:35.139 --> 00:24:37.709
<i> which makes it
 a vestigial reflex,</i>

00:24:37.742 --> 00:24:40.912
<i> something we don't need anymore
 but we still have.</i>

00:24:40.945 --> 00:24:44.782
<i> It is known as
 the Piloerection Reflex</i>

00:24:44.815 --> 00:24:49.087
or, more commonly,
goosebumps.

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:53.858
<i> Anything that's different
 or abnormal can be scary.</i>

00:24:53.891 --> 00:24:56.561 line:0%
Take it, Georgie.

00:24:56.594 --> 00:24:58.029 line:0%
[Michael]
<i> Like Pennywise in "It,"</i>

00:24:58.062 --> 00:25:00.598 line:0%
<i> oftentimes the scariest
 type of predator</i>

00:25:00.631 --> 00:25:02.567 line:0%
<i> isn't a human, a creature,
 or a beast,</i>

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:05.803 line:0%
<i> it's a variation
 of a human being...</i>

00:25:05.836 --> 00:25:07.639
[growling]

00:25:07.672 --> 00:25:11.175
<i> ...a distorted human form
 that looks, acts,</i>

00:25:11.208 --> 00:25:14.746 line:0%
<i> or even moves in
 a disturbingly unnatural way.</i>

00:25:14.779 --> 00:25:16.281
Oh, my God.

00:25:16.314 --> 00:25:19.817 align:start size:63% position:38%
One thing many of us
are terrified by

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:21.886 align:start size:81% position:19%
is a distorted human form.

00:25:21.919 --> 00:25:23.154 align:start size:28% position:72%
Why?

00:25:23.187 --> 00:25:25.189 align:start position:0%
Our brain and our mind

00:25:25.222 --> 00:25:27.091 align:start position:0%
is a kind of
prediction processor.

00:25:27.124 --> 00:25:29.093 align:start position:0%
We're trying to predict
what's gonna happen next.

00:25:29.126 --> 00:25:31.829 align:start position:0%
So when anything comes in
and it doesn't match
our template,

00:25:31.862 --> 00:25:33.665 align:start position:0%
or sense of categories,

00:25:33.698 --> 00:25:36.935 align:start position:0%
then we're cognitively
and emotionally aroused.

00:25:36.968 --> 00:25:40.004
So it activates the amygdala,
it makes us a little suspicious

00:25:40.037 --> 00:25:41.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
or at least puts us
on our toes again.

00:25:41.906 --> 00:25:44.876
We have a genetic predisposition

00:25:44.909 --> 00:25:48.646
to be averse to,
or at least aroused by,

00:25:48.679 --> 00:25:51.316
that which is outside
the ordinary.

00:25:51.349 --> 00:25:53.651
But when you're fresh
out of the womb,

00:25:53.684 --> 00:25:57.689
you don't know what's normal
or what's distorted and scary.

00:25:57.722 --> 00:26:00.725
Researchers are Princeton
and Florida Atlantic University

00:26:00.758 --> 00:26:03.761
demonstrated this
by showing normal faces

00:26:03.794 --> 00:26:07.899
and creepy, distorted faces...
to babies.

00:26:07.932 --> 00:26:10.101
<i> Children that were at least
 one year old</i>

00:26:10.134 --> 00:26:13.171 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> avoided looking
 at the scary faces,
 which makes sense.</i>

00:26:13.204 --> 00:26:15.006
<i> But younger children,</i>

00:26:15.039 --> 00:26:17.775
<i> like those who were
 only eight months old, didn't.</i>

00:26:17.808 --> 00:26:19.844
<i> The hypothesis is
 that at that age,</i>

00:26:19.877 --> 00:26:23.214
<i> normal hasn't yet been
 sufficiently established.</i>

00:26:23.247 --> 00:26:26.284
<i> A creepy face is just
 another thing to look at.</i>

00:26:26.317 --> 00:26:30.355 align:start size:88% position:13%
It only becomes creepy
when you've been around
long enough to know

00:26:30.388 --> 00:26:32.223
what's<i> not</i> creepy.

00:26:32.256 --> 00:26:34.959
So when it comes
to finding the one thing

00:26:34.992 --> 00:26:38.730
that can panic anyone,
I'll need to look elsewhere.

00:26:38.763 --> 00:26:41.165
Because if a newborn baby
rolls in here,

00:26:41.198 --> 00:26:42.967
I won't be able to scare it

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:47.038
even with the most vile,
scary, unnatural face.

00:26:49.807 --> 00:26:51.976
Of course,
it's not just faces.

00:26:52.009 --> 00:26:55.079
Distorted natural forms
also include bodies,

00:26:55.112 --> 00:26:58.182
like humans
with other animal parts.

00:26:58.215 --> 00:27:00.652
[creature growling]

00:27:02.153 --> 00:27:05.790
Or creatures moving unnaturally.

00:27:07.058 --> 00:27:09.127
Oh, my.

00:27:12.897 --> 00:27:15.066
<i> Oh, my.</i>

00:27:15.099 --> 00:27:16.734 align:start size:53% position:47%
Hoo hoo hoo!

00:27:16.767 --> 00:27:19.037 align:start size:63% position:38%
"Oh, my," indeed.

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:21.139
You know what's going on here,

00:27:21.172 --> 00:27:23.708
this is the third origin.

00:27:23.741 --> 00:27:26.811
Fears can be born through
firsthand experiences,

00:27:26.844 --> 00:27:28.413
like feeling pain,

00:27:28.446 --> 00:27:31.215
or informationally,
through stories,

00:27:31.248 --> 00:27:34.786
but we can also learn fear
vicariously

00:27:34.819 --> 00:27:38.056
by watching others
experience fear.

00:27:38.089 --> 00:27:41.092 line:0%
[Michael]<i> It's called
 Social Fear Transmission,</i>

00:27:41.125 --> 00:27:44.462 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
<i> and we tried it out out
 during my fear conditioning
 demonstration.</i>

00:27:44.495 --> 00:27:48.700 line:0%
<i> Our unknowing subject was
 my good friend Alie Ward.</i>

00:27:48.733 --> 00:27:51.169 line:0%
<i> Alie was invited to observe
 my experience</i>

00:27:51.202 --> 00:27:53.337 line:0%
<i> and witness my reactions.</i>

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:55.873 align:start size:91% position:9%
- [electricity crackling,
recorded scream]
- [Michael] Jeez!

00:27:55.906 --> 00:27:59.911 align:start size:94% position:6%
Okay, little bit more scared
of the square right now.

00:27:59.944 --> 00:28:01.746
[Michael]
<i> Right after my test,</i>

00:28:01.779 --> 00:28:04.015
<i> it was her turn to face
 the green circle</i>

00:28:04.048 --> 00:28:05.883
<i> and the purple square</i>

00:28:05.916 --> 00:28:09.253
<i> that appeared
 whenever I got shocked.</i>

00:28:09.286 --> 00:28:11.923 align:start size:75% position:25%
[Dr. Zbozinek]
We're gonna use the same
shock intensity

00:28:11.956 --> 00:28:13.424 align:start size:81% position:19%
- that Michael had.
- Okay.

00:28:13.457 --> 00:28:15.893
[Michael]
<i> Except, that wasn't true.</i>

00:28:15.926 --> 00:28:19.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
<i> Alie's test would not
 include any shocks
 or startling sounds.</i>

00:28:19.964 --> 00:28:23.735 align:start position:0%
[Alie] Is it normal to be
shaking before this? [laughs]

00:28:23.768 --> 00:28:27.038 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
Yeah, it's an anxiety-provoking
task. Are you feeling okay

00:28:27.071 --> 00:28:29.474 line:0%
- about doing it?
- Mmm-hmm. Sure.

00:28:29.507 --> 00:28:31.743 align:start size:84% position:16%
[Michael]
<i> Would Alie be afraid
 of the purple square</i>

00:28:31.776 --> 00:28:34.245
<i> just from observing
 my fear of it?</i>

00:28:34.278 --> 00:28:36.180 align:start size:78% position:22%
- You ready, Alie?
- Mmm-hmm.

00:28:36.213 --> 00:28:37.348 align:start size:72% position:28%
All righty, here we go.

00:28:48.826 --> 00:28:51.195
I feel like I'm gonna barf.

00:28:51.228 --> 00:28:53.531
[Michael]
<i> Alie already seems scared.</i>

00:28:53.564 --> 00:28:56.434
<i> But is she just
 afraid in general?</i>

00:28:56.467 --> 00:29:00.304
<i> Or is she more afraid
 of the purple square?</i>

00:29:00.337 --> 00:29:02.974
<i> Which shape
 is making her sweat the most?</i>

00:29:14.185 --> 00:29:15.920 align:start size:78% position:22%
- That's it?
- You're all done.

00:29:15.953 --> 00:29:18.489 align:start size:91% position:9%
The shock fairy
did not come to visit me?

00:29:18.522 --> 00:29:20.825 align:start size:72% position:28%
The shock fairy did not
come to visit you.

00:29:20.858 --> 00:29:24.028 align:start size:91% position:9%
[laughing]
So much adrenaline
for no pain.

00:29:24.061 --> 00:29:29.066 align:start position:0%
My hands are sweating so much
that the dye on my pants
is, like, on my hands now.

00:29:29.099 --> 00:29:31.936 align:start size:78% position:22%
[Michael]
<i> So which shape
 scared Alie more?</i>

00:29:33.437 --> 00:29:36.107 line:0%
<i> Despite receiving
 no adverse stimuli,</i>

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:38.442 line:0%
<i> but after observing</i> my<i> fear,</i>

00:29:38.475 --> 00:29:41.479 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> she had a higher
 physiological response
 to the purple square</i>

00:29:41.512 --> 00:29:43.014
<i> than the green circle.</i>

00:29:43.047 --> 00:29:44.515 align:start size:59% position:41%
You were definitely
more afraid

00:29:44.548 --> 00:29:46.484 align:start size:81% position:19%
of that purple square
throughout the experiment.

00:29:46.517 --> 00:29:48.352
Ahh. You know,
I'm not a gambler,

00:29:48.385 --> 00:29:50.154
but I think I was thinking,

00:29:50.187 --> 00:29:52.190
"I haven't gotten shocked yet--
it's coming."

00:29:52.223 --> 00:29:53.958
People are always saying
that they learn from me,

00:29:53.991 --> 00:29:55.326
but it's usually, you know,

00:29:55.359 --> 00:29:57.528 align:start size:91% position:9%
math or science
or facts in general.

00:29:57.561 --> 00:29:59.530 align:start size:91% position:9%
You learned<i> fear.</i>

00:29:59.563 --> 00:30:01.465 align:start size:63% position:38%
I still hate
that purple square.

00:30:01.498 --> 00:30:02.567
You're welcome.

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:05.136 align:start size:50% position:50%
[laughing]

00:30:07.304 --> 00:30:10.474
Let's organize
what we've learned so far.

00:30:10.507 --> 00:30:14.078
Fear is a feeling
that we learn to have

00:30:14.111 --> 00:30:16.948
in response to things
our amygdala figures

00:30:16.981 --> 00:30:19.150
we would do best to avoid

00:30:19.183 --> 00:30:22.620
because in one way or another,
they could lead to our demise.

00:30:22.653 --> 00:30:26.123
Strands of fear-silk
have connected them in some way

00:30:26.156 --> 00:30:28.192
to possible death.

00:30:28.225 --> 00:30:31.495
Now, from talking with experts,
in my estimation,

00:30:31.528 --> 00:30:33.898 line:0%
there are eight
unique innate aversions

00:30:33.931 --> 00:30:37.034 line:0%
selected to engender panic
in us, by evolution,

00:30:37.067 --> 00:30:38.469 line:0%
over millions of years.

00:30:38.502 --> 00:30:41.305
They come pre-learned
in our DNA,

00:30:41.338 --> 00:30:44.575 line:0%
and include pain, isolation,

00:30:44.608 --> 00:30:46.577 line:0%
the unknown or abnormal,

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:49.447 line:0%
disease, sudden movement,

00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:51.949
suffocation, falling,

00:30:51.982 --> 00:30:54.285
and incapacitation.

00:30:54.318 --> 00:30:58.222
These form the inner ring
of the web of fear.

00:30:58.255 --> 00:31:02.260
All our fears,
from rats to radiation,

00:31:02.293 --> 00:31:06.163
are based on connections
made by our amygdalae to death

00:31:06.196 --> 00:31:09.400
through one or more
of these innate aversions.

00:31:09.433 --> 00:31:11.502
When it comes to learned fears,

00:31:11.535 --> 00:31:14.639
there may be no limit
to the number we can acquire.

00:31:14.672 --> 00:31:17.675
As long as your amygdala
keeps spinning its threads,

00:31:17.708 --> 00:31:20.278
the web can extend forever.

00:31:20.311 --> 00:31:22.346
But here's what's even cooler:

00:31:22.379 --> 00:31:25.549
when our learned fears
are combined just right,

00:31:25.582 --> 00:31:28.953
they can be scarier
than the sum of their parts.

00:31:28.986 --> 00:31:31.222
Researchers have found
that if you've been conditioned

00:31:31.255 --> 00:31:32.924
to fear two separate stimuli,

00:31:32.957 --> 00:31:35.693
say, a purple square
and a green circle,

00:31:35.726 --> 00:31:39.563
seeing them suddenly together
makes you expect a worse outcome

00:31:39.596 --> 00:31:42.466
than seeing either stimulus
individually.

00:31:42.499 --> 00:31:45.102 line:0%
- [Sidney screams]
-<i> Horror filmmakers</i>

00:31:45.135 --> 00:31:47.171 line:0%
<i> take advantage
 of this phenomenon</i>

00:31:47.204 --> 00:31:48.572 line:0%
<i> by employing a technique</i>

00:31:48.605 --> 00:31:51.309 align:start size:56% position:44%
<i> called
 Category Jamming,</i>

00:31:51.342 --> 00:31:54.412 align:start size:50% position:50%
<i> mashing several
 categories of
 fears together</i>

00:31:54.445 --> 00:31:57.448 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
<i> to create
 the scariest
 villains possible.</i>

00:31:57.481 --> 00:32:00.651 size:81% position:41% line:0%
[Dr. Asma]<i> There's people
 like Freddy Kreuger,</i>

00:32:00.684 --> 00:32:04.088 line:0%
where you've got many things
that indicate death and fear,

00:32:04.121 --> 00:32:06.490 align:start position:0%
<i> like, you know,
 the mottled flesh of his skin,</i>

00:32:06.523 --> 00:32:09.060 size:75% position:38%
<i> the burn, he's a ghost,</i>

00:32:09.093 --> 00:32:11.595 align:start position:0%
<i> his hands are blades
 of some kind.</i>

00:32:11.628 --> 00:32:13.631 align:start position:0% line:0%
<i> You're weaving together
 a number</i>

00:32:13.664 --> 00:32:17.168 align:start position:0% line:0%
<i> of very fearful associations
 that most people have.</i>

00:32:17.201 --> 00:32:19.537 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> - And his criminal past.
 - Right. All that together.</i>

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:21.272 align:start size:88% position:13%
<i> Exactly.</i>

00:32:21.305 --> 00:32:23.708 line:0%
[squishy sounds,
whiplash sounds]

00:32:23.741 --> 00:32:27.411 size:84% position:42% line:0%
If you look at, sort of,
the<i> Alien</i> franchise, you'll
see that the face hugger

00:32:27.444 --> 00:32:32.416 align:start position:0%
<i> that grabs onto your
 face is a beautiful hybrid
 of a spider and a snake.</i>

00:32:32.449 --> 00:32:35.119 align:start position:0%
<i> Those are two things
 that are universally feared,</i>

00:32:35.152 --> 00:32:37.388 align:start position:0%
<i> and so this is
 a kind of category jamming.</i>

00:32:37.421 --> 00:32:39.323 align:start position:0%
<i> It doesn't fit in
 our categories.</i>

00:32:39.356 --> 00:32:41.459 align:start position:0%
The designers
basically put them together

00:32:41.492 --> 00:32:44.528 align:start position:0%
so the fear
is amplified tremendously.

00:32:44.561 --> 00:32:47.365 align:start position:0%
Another example could be
just taking something

00:32:47.398 --> 00:32:50.301 align:start position:0%
that ordinarily
would never be here
and putting it there.

00:32:50.334 --> 00:32:52.503 align:start position:0% line:0%
If you put snakes
on a plane,

00:32:52.536 --> 00:32:55.039 align:start position:0% line:0%
<i> you've got something
 most people are afraid of--
 snakes--</i>

00:32:55.072 --> 00:32:57.041 align:start position:0% line:0%
<i> you've got flying,
 which people are afraid of.</i>

00:32:57.074 --> 00:32:58.776 align:start position:0%
<i> You've also got
 strange domain crossing.</i>

00:32:58.809 --> 00:33:01.679 align:start position:0%
Why would snakes be on a plane?
That doesn't make sense.

00:33:01.712 --> 00:33:04.015 align:start position:0%
And that's unsettling
in and of itself.

00:33:04.048 --> 00:33:08.386 align:start position:0%
So you're weaving together
a number of very fearful
associations

00:33:08.419 --> 00:33:11.656 align:start position:0%
that most people have,
and that's uniquely disturbing,
I think.

00:33:13.357 --> 00:33:16.727
So maybe<i> extreme</i> 
category jamming,

00:33:16.760 --> 00:33:21.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
combining every
innate aversion
and conditioned fear,

00:33:21.198 --> 00:33:24.568
could be the scariest thing.

00:33:24.601 --> 00:33:29.240
It would certainly be likely to
catch anyone in a web of fear.

00:33:29.273 --> 00:33:31.475
Let's try it out.

00:33:31.508 --> 00:33:34.678
Now, conveniently,
I'm already in a graveyard,

00:33:34.711 --> 00:33:39.316
so we already have
the fear of death, decay...

00:33:39.349 --> 00:33:40.518
<i> [church bells tolling]</i>

00:33:40.551 --> 00:33:44.288
...it's dark, I'm alone.

00:33:44.321 --> 00:33:47.558
Let's add in
some scary sounds...

00:33:47.591 --> 00:33:49.427
<i> [wolf howling]</i>

00:33:49.460 --> 00:33:51.495
[growling]

00:33:51.528 --> 00:33:54.165
...a distorted human form,

00:33:54.198 --> 00:33:56.200
sudden movement...

00:33:57.734 --> 00:33:59.704
a mortal threat...

00:34:02.706 --> 00:34:04.408
gore...

00:34:04.441 --> 00:34:07.445
[creature growling]

00:34:07.478 --> 00:34:09.780
story and context...

00:34:09.813 --> 00:34:12.316
[growling]

00:34:14.485 --> 00:34:15.720
[growls]

00:34:18.155 --> 00:34:20.691
...incapacitation.

00:34:20.724 --> 00:34:21.759
Is this it?

00:34:21.792 --> 00:34:23.861
Is the scariest thing possible

00:34:23.894 --> 00:34:27.098
just a combination
of every innate aversion

00:34:27.131 --> 00:34:29.233
and conditioned fear?

00:34:29.266 --> 00:34:31.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
[laughing]

00:34:31.201 --> 00:34:33.204 align:start position:0%
Oh, dear, Michael.

00:34:33.237 --> 00:34:36.440 align:start position:0%
Conditioned fears
know no bounds.

00:34:36.473 --> 00:34:39.844 align:start position:0%
So the scariest thing
is that the human mind

00:34:39.877 --> 00:34:43.280
can be made afraid of<i> anything!</i>

00:34:43.313 --> 00:34:45.216 align:start position:0%
[laughs evilly]

00:34:45.249 --> 00:34:48.185 align:start size:38% position:63%
No! Noooo!

00:34:48.218 --> 00:34:49.587
[Michael 3] 
<i> Hold it!</i>

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:51.422 align:start size:78% position:22%
Both of you, stop. Just--

00:34:51.455 --> 00:34:53.324
Let's take a step back.

00:34:53.357 --> 00:34:57.161
Category jamming together
every possible learned fear

00:34:57.194 --> 00:34:59.530
and every innate aversion

00:34:59.563 --> 00:35:01.298
just sounds like a cop-out.

00:35:01.331 --> 00:35:04.735
I don't like that.
I want to be more specific.

00:35:04.768 --> 00:35:07.705
But this idea that
the scariest thing

00:35:07.738 --> 00:35:09.707
is the fact that
the human mind

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:11.809
can be made afraid
of anything--

00:35:11.842 --> 00:35:14.178
I don't like that, either.

00:35:14.211 --> 00:35:18.282
I mean, it might be
the scariest thing possible...

00:35:18.315 --> 00:35:20.718
but it also might not.

00:35:20.751 --> 00:35:24.822
What about people
who don't have an amygdala?

00:35:24.855 --> 00:35:27.758
Meet Patient SM.

00:35:27.791 --> 00:35:30.594
<i> Not the snake, the hands.</i>

00:35:30.627 --> 00:35:33.397
<i> This is all we are allowed
 to show you.</i> 

00:35:33.430 --> 00:35:36.167
<i> Her amygdala was destroyed
 at the age of ten</i>

00:35:36.200 --> 00:35:38.536
<i> due to an incredibly rare
 genetic disorder.</i>

00:35:38.569 --> 00:35:40.704
And because she doesn't
have an amygdala,

00:35:40.737 --> 00:35:44.441
she cannot learn to register
experiences as threats,

00:35:44.474 --> 00:35:46.744
which puts her life in danger,

00:35:46.777 --> 00:35:49.380
and that's why
we can't reveal her identity.

00:35:49.413 --> 00:35:52.516 align:start position:0% line:0%
Patient SM's feelings,
the feeling states,

00:35:52.549 --> 00:35:56.287 align:start position:0%
her subjective states,
seem to be absent in Patient SM.

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:59.223 align:start position:0% line:0%
For example,
when she was walking
through a park one night,

00:35:59.256 --> 00:36:01.759 align:start position:0% line:0%
a guy came up to her
and put a knife to her throat,

00:36:01.792 --> 00:36:03.661
saying, "Give me your money."

00:36:03.694 --> 00:36:05.462 align:start position:0%
And she just kind
of ignored them and said,

00:36:05.495 --> 00:36:09.233 align:start position:0%
"Well, if you cut me,
I'll chase you down
and kill you."

00:36:09.266 --> 00:36:10.968 size:63% position:69%
She was just
<i> all</i> rational.

00:36:11.001 --> 00:36:13.904 align:start position:0%
Yeah, yeah.
And she apparently
kind of knows

00:36:13.937 --> 00:36:16.240 align:start position:0%
that this isn't right,

00:36:16.273 --> 00:36:19.610 align:start position:0%
but she reacts
in a different way.

00:36:19.643 --> 00:36:21.879
Researchers have even
purposefully exposed her

00:36:21.912 --> 00:36:24.949
to classically scary things,
like spiders and snakes

00:36:24.982 --> 00:36:26.584
and haunted houses
and horror films,

00:36:26.617 --> 00:36:28.852
and she has exhibited
no fear response

00:36:28.885 --> 00:36:31.455
to any of them whatsoever.

00:36:31.488 --> 00:36:35.593
For a long time, scientists
considered SM living proof

00:36:35.626 --> 00:36:39.463
that without an amygdala,
we are unable to feel fear.

00:36:39.496 --> 00:36:40.798
But then...

00:36:40.831 --> 00:36:44.335
one experiment
changed everything.

00:36:44.368 --> 00:36:46.704
Scientists have known
for decades

00:36:46.737 --> 00:36:50.241
that a lack of oxygen
will make you feel dizzy,

00:36:50.274 --> 00:36:54.311
and maybe even euphoric,
before you pass out.

00:36:54.344 --> 00:36:58.015
But elevated levels
of carbon dioxide in the blood

00:36:58.048 --> 00:37:01.852
induce fear, anxiety, and panic.

00:37:01.885 --> 00:37:07.491
Patient SM volunteered
to inhale concentrated CO2.

00:37:07.524 --> 00:37:11.829 align:start size:91% position:9%
Her brain interpreted
the elevated levels of CO2
in her blood

00:37:11.862 --> 00:37:13.964
as proof that
she was suffocating,

00:37:13.997 --> 00:37:17.635
<i> a process
 we are innately averse to.</i>

00:37:17.668 --> 00:37:22.773
<i> As usual, the researchers
 expected SM to feel no fear.</i>

00:37:22.806 --> 00:37:25.576
But for the first time
in her adult life,

00:37:25.609 --> 00:37:28.779
<i> she felt sheer and utter panic.</i>

00:37:31.014 --> 00:37:33.684 align:start position:0%
The great thing
about that study on SM

00:37:33.717 --> 00:37:35.419
is really it was the first time

00:37:35.452 --> 00:37:38.355 align:start position:0%
that we showed that
these emotional responses

00:37:38.388 --> 00:37:39.857
can occur without an amygdala.

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:42.059
Over the course
of the next year,

00:37:42.092 --> 00:37:45.396
the scientists tracked down
two rare patients in Germany

00:37:45.429 --> 00:37:48.299
who also had
non-functioning amygdalae

00:37:48.332 --> 00:37:51.602
and gave them the same
CO2 inhalation test.

00:37:51.635 --> 00:37:53.837
They had
the same panic response.

00:37:53.870 --> 00:37:55.773 line:0%
<i> Researchers believe</i>

00:37:55.806 --> 00:37:58.542 line:0%
<i> that the fight-or-flight
 responses they observed</i>

00:37:58.575 --> 00:38:00.477 line:0%
<i> in these amygdala-damaged
 patients</i>

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:03.314 line:0%
<i> were induced by
 CO2-sensing neurons</i>

00:38:03.347 --> 00:38:06.950 line:0%
<i> in evolutionarily older regions
 of the brain,</i>

00:38:06.983 --> 00:38:10.321 line:0%
<i> like the midbrain
 or even the brain stem,</i>

00:38:10.354 --> 00:38:13.590 line:0%
<i> which control more primal
 physiological functions.</i>

00:38:13.623 --> 00:38:16.727 line:0%
<i> And so if I want to terrify</i>

00:38:16.760 --> 00:38:19.897
even the neurologically
un-scare-able,

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:23.667
I need our body's
internal fear response

00:38:23.700 --> 00:38:27.404
to elevated carbon dioxide
levels in the blood.

00:38:27.437 --> 00:38:29.606
But the story doesn't end there.

00:38:29.639 --> 00:38:33.010 align:start size:88% position:13%
SM's researchers
later discovered
something even weirder.

00:38:33.043 --> 00:38:37.548 align:start size:91% position:9%
<i> When a control group
 of people with functioning
 amygdalae inhaled CO2,</i>

00:38:37.581 --> 00:38:40.784
<i> they showed a lower
 fear reaction than Patient SM.</i>

00:38:40.817 --> 00:38:45.389 align:start size:88% position:13%
Why? Well, this question
led to a potentially
revolutionary rethinking

00:38:45.422 --> 00:38:47.524
of the amygdala's role in fear.

00:38:47.557 --> 00:38:51.362 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
Researches still believe
that the amygdala learns
to detect threats

00:38:51.395 --> 00:38:53.464 line:0%
<i> that are outside the body</i>

00:38:53.497 --> 00:38:55.432 line:0%
<i> so that the body
 can respond to them,</i>

00:38:55.465 --> 00:38:57.935
<i> but when it comes
 to</i> internal<i> threats,</i>

00:38:57.968 --> 00:39:02.406
<i> it may actually inhibit
 your fear response.</i>

00:39:02.439 --> 00:39:05.008
<i> The control subjects
 knew they were participating</i>

00:39:05.041 --> 00:39:07.778
<i> in a safe,
 voluntary experiment,</i>

00:39:07.811 --> 00:39:11.415 align:start size:94% position:6%
<i> so their amygdalae
 may have effectively told
 other parts of their brain,</i>

00:39:11.448 --> 00:39:13.851
"Hey, relax.
Things are bad in the body,

00:39:13.884 --> 00:39:17.087 align:start size:88% position:13%
"but I don't detect
a real external threat,
so, like, calm down.

00:39:17.120 --> 00:39:20.124
A panic response
isn't gonna help;
not warranted."

00:39:20.157 --> 00:39:25.896 align:start size:91% position:9%
So if you want to ensure
the utmost amount of panic
in the most people,

00:39:25.929 --> 00:39:27.998
you don't need to category jam

00:39:28.031 --> 00:39:31.835
every possible scary
and unpleasant thing together,

00:39:31.868 --> 00:39:33.871
you only need two of them.

00:39:33.904 --> 00:39:37.007
Increase a person's
blood CO2 levels

00:39:37.040 --> 00:39:41.044
and present an external threat
they'll believe is causing it.

00:39:41.077 --> 00:39:43.580
Drowning. Waterboarding.

00:39:43.613 --> 00:39:45.549
Coerced CO2 inhalation.

00:39:45.582 --> 00:39:51.522
They're all variations
of the one true scariest thing:

00:39:51.555 --> 00:39:55.025
<i> elevation of carbon dioxide
 in the blood</i>

00:39:55.058 --> 00:39:58.429
<i> caused by an uncontrollable
 external threat.</i>

00:40:00.096 --> 00:40:01.465
[snickers]

00:40:01.498 --> 00:40:03.133
Sweet dreams.

00:40:03.166 --> 00:40:05.502
And as always...

00:40:05.535 --> 00:40:07.171
thanks for watching.

